      "id": 1,
      "text": "Cheung Sha Wan means \"Long Sandy Bay\" and was once a beach frequented by Tanka fishermen.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheung_Sha_Wan",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Lei Cheng Uk Han Tomb in Cheung Sha Wan dates to AD 25‑220, the oldest known constructed structure in Hong Kong.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheung_Sha_Wan",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1950s, Cheung Sha Wan Road hosted a textile empire, with factories like Hong Kong Spinners covering over 300,000 sq ft.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cheung-sha-wan-road.hk/cheung-sha-wan-road-industrial-heritage-guide",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Wang Cheong Factory Estate, built in 1984, still houses 1,020 units and illustrates the area’s ongoing industrial legacy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cheung-sha-wan-road.hk/cheung-sha-wan-road-industrial-heritage-guide",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Cheung Sha Wan station on the MTR Tsuen Wan line lies east of the area, while Lai Chi Kok station, actually in Cheung Sha Wan, serves the west.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheung_Sha_Wan",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Former factory buildings now host artist studios and cultural spaces, such as the JCCAC vertical artist village, reflecting a creative economy shift.",
